Subject: [PATCH] dmaengine: xilinx_dma: bound zero-delay atomic polling

Linux 6.18 atomic polling with delay_us=0 consumes one nominal nanosecond
per iteration. A 1000000-us Xilinx timeout can therefore busy-loop through
about one billion MMIO reads. DAPHNE S2MM abort on an incomplete packet took
34.95 seconds and raised a soft lockup in xilinx_dma_stop_transfer.

Use a minimum 1-us retry delay for this driver wrapper. The first condition
check remains immediate. Stop, reset and start polling share the same fix;
existing explicitly nonzero delays are preserved. The isolated provider
built from the exact deployed source completed active abort in 1.295 seconds
and passed STOP, timeout, SIGTERM, SIGKILL and reopen on all three S2MM paths.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
@@ -531,7 +531,7 @@
 	container_of(tx, struct xilinx_dma_tx_descriptor, async_tx)
 #define xilinx_dma_poll_timeout(chan, reg, val, cond, delay_us, timeout_us) \
 	readl_poll_timeout_atomic(chan->xdev->regs + chan->ctrl_offset + reg, \
-				  val, cond, delay_us, timeout_us)
+				  val, cond, max_t(unsigned long, delay_us, 1), timeout_us)
 
 /* IO accessors */
 static inline u32 dma_read(struct xilinx_dma_chan *chan, u32 reg)
